-- title:
--   Free zero-dimensional Polish actions admit small-step support-space orbit maps
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathbb Z^d$ act freely and continuously on a zero-dimensional Polish space $X$. Let $H$ be a finite graph with support torsion data $D$, and fix a coordinate $i$ and $\varepsilon>0$. There exists a map $F:X\times\mathbb Z^d\to\mathcal S(H)$ such that its origin map is continuous with finite range, and
--   $$F(g\cdot x,0)=\tau^{\,g_i\bmod2}F(x,g),$$
--   $$d(F(x,g+e_j),F(x,g))<\varepsilon
--   \qquad(x\in X,\ g\in\mathbb Z^d,\ 1\le j\le d).$$
--   Here $\mathcal S(H)$ is the space of compatible pairs of probability masses, and $\tau$ swaps the two shores. The chosen coordinate entails $d\ge1$.
-- source:
--   Ruijun Wang, Continuous graph homomorphisms of higher dimensional abelian group actions, arXiv:2609.39824v1 (30 September 2026), Sections 2–7, supporting construction for Theorem 1.4 and Proposition 7.1; https://arxiv.org/html/2609.39824v1. Formalization-specific helper: AbelianTargets/SmallStepProjectedOrbitMaps.lean, lines 30–82; SHA256 bd81904c3fa0f31edda36fc3e5eb2a6e4ff0febf612173545c4391470bf297ed.

